1
00:00:02,135 --> 00:00:04,736
So we had a look at resolving collisions

2
00:00:04,768 --> 00:00:05,699
with chaining.

3
00:00:06,293 --> 00:00:08,862
We also can resolve collisions with a

4
00:00:08,862 --> 00:00:11,222
different strategy. Instead of chaining,

5
00:00:11,816 --> 00:00:15,317
we could also use open addressing. Now what

6
00:00:15,365 --> 00:00:15,975
does this mean?

7
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:18,142
This simply means

8
00:00:18,736 --> 00:00:21,562
that for our hash table, if we generate the

9
00:00:21,562 --> 00:00:23,168
same hash for a different key,

10
00:00:23,874 --> 00:00:26,506
we don't add it as an extra value for that

11
00:00:26,506 --> 00:00:28,510
given key, for that given hash.

12
00:00:29,087 --> 00:00:31,620
Instead, we simply look for the next empty

13
00:00:32,037 --> 00:00:35,002
index in our hash table and then store the

14
00:00:35,018 --> 00:00:37,952
value there. So we search the next empty

15
00:00:38,064 --> 00:00:40,180
bucket to then store the value there.

16
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:44,092
Now, this is called open addressing and

17
00:00:44,092 --> 00:00:47,249
there are different ways of finding the next

18
00:00:47,362 --> 00:00:50,247
empty slot. We can simply go through all the

19
00:00:50,247 --> 00:00:52,780
indexes and take the first one that's empty

20
00:00:53,357 --> 00:00:57,348
or we make bigger jumps to also look into

21
00:00:57,381 --> 00:01:00,809
the later parts of our hash table and find

22
00:01:00,826 --> 00:01:01,883
empty spots there.

23
00:01:02,812 --> 00:01:05,344
That can help with efficiently storing the

24
00:01:05,376 --> 00:01:07,539
hash table in memory then, for example.

25
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:10,743
Either way, no matter how we find the next

26
00:01:10,759 --> 00:01:13,707
empty bucket, it is about finding that next

27
00:01:13,723 --> 00:01:14,012
bucket.

28
00:01:14,604 --> 00:01:16,495
And therefore, of course, it's obvious that

29
00:01:16,527 --> 00:01:19,299
this approach could fail if we have a very

30
00:01:19,331 --> 00:01:22,824
small hash table and there simply are no

31
00:01:22,936 --> 00:01:26,156
empty spaces left. So this needs a bigger

32
00:01:26,204 --> 00:01:29,506
hash table And of course also a certain way

33
00:01:29,506 --> 00:01:31,253
of building it such that

34
00:01:31,814 --> 00:01:35,501
unused spaces, for example, are freed up so

35
00:01:35,501 --> 00:01:37,665
that we're not keeping values which are not

36
00:01:37,681 --> 00:01:40,422
getting used anymore. Because if we free up

37
00:01:40,630 --> 00:01:42,986
values, if we free up keys and their

38
00:01:43,195 --> 00:01:45,743
indexes, then of course we ensure that those

39
00:01:46,016 --> 00:01:48,613
buckets, those slots can be taken again.

40
00:01:49,158 --> 00:01:50,825
So here we'll have to make sure that we

41
00:01:50,905 --> 00:01:53,309
don't run out of space, which is a problem

42
00:01:53,309 --> 00:01:55,715
we didn't have to deal with if we used the

43
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:56,582
chaining approach.

44
00:01:57,385 --> 00:01:59,489
Nonetheless, let's see how we can implement

45
00:01:59,489 --> 00:02:02,106
a simplified version of open addressing

46
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:04,739
before we then also dive into

47
00:02:05,494 --> 00:02:07,629
the different approaches we can use, into

48
00:02:07,678 --> 00:02:09,010
the time complexities,

49
00:02:09,669 --> 00:02:12,703
and we derive what we should do in reality

50
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:13,587
when writing code.

51
00:02:14,598 --> 00:02:17,408
So back in code, I stored the chaining

52
00:02:17,408 --> 00:02:18,564
approach in a new file,

53
00:02:19,239 --> 00:02:21,246
and now in app.js, I'll again start with the

54
00:02:21,246 --> 00:02:24,375
basic hash table code and this testing code

55
00:02:24,375 --> 00:02:26,909
from down there and put this into an empty

56
00:02:27,005 --> 00:02:29,074
app.js file again so that we can again

57
00:02:29,491 --> 00:02:32,298
implement this from scratch and now work

58
00:02:32,298 --> 00:02:34,447
with the open addressing solution.

59
00:02:35,538 --> 00:02:37,815
So how could we implement open addressing?

60
00:02:38,794 --> 00:02:41,713
In the end, when we set a value, instead of

61
00:02:41,761 --> 00:02:43,830
just inserting the value

62
00:02:44,487 --> 00:02:47,374
in the place for the generated key hash, we

63
00:02:47,374 --> 00:02:50,598
wanna check if that spot is available, if we

